Hereâs a proven pattern for âexportingâ the Az PowerShell module (and all of its dependencies) from an Internetâconnected machine, copying it to an offline machine, and âinstallingâ it there:

 1. On your Internet-connected server

    a. Open an elevated PowerShell session (run as Administrator).  
    b. Create a folder to stage your modules.  
       ```powershell
       $staging = 'C:\OfflinePSModules'
       if (!(Test-Path $staging)) { New-Item -Path $staging -ItemType Directory | Out-Null }
       ```  
    c. Use Save-Module (PowerShellGet v2+) to pull down Az and its dependencies:  
       ```powershell
       Save-Module -Name Az `
                   -Repository PSGallery `
                   -Path $staging `
                   -IncludeDependencies `
                   -Force
       ```
       This will create subfolders in C:\OfflinePSModules\Az, Az.Accounts, Az.Resources, etc.

    d. (Optional but recommended) Grab the latest PowerShellGet, PackageManagement and the NuGet provider, so your offline box wonât be missing them:
       ```powershell
       Save-Module -Name PowerShellGet,PackageManagement `
                   -Repository PSGallery `
                   -Path $staging `
                   -Force

       # NuGet provider is a âpackage provider,â not a module:
       Save-PackageProvider -Name NuGet -Path $staging -Force
       ```

    e. Copy the entire contents of C:\OfflinePSModules to your offline machine (USB drive, ISO, file share, etc.).

 2. On your Offline server

    a. Pick a permanent location for your offline repoâe.g. D:\PSRepoâand copy all of the folders under C:\OfflinePSModules here.
    b. Open an elevated PowerShell session.
    c. (Option A) Register your local folder as a repository and use Install-Module:

       ```powershell
       Register-PSRepository -Name LocalPSRepo `
                             -SourceLocation 'D:\PSRepo' `
                             -InstallationPolicy Trusted

       # If PowerShellGet or PackageManagement arenât yet installed on this box,
       # install them first from your local repo:
       Install-Module -Name PowerShellGet,PackageManagement `
                      -Repository LocalPSRepo `
                      -Force

       # Ensure the NuGet provider is available (bootstrap from your local path):
       Install-PackageProvider -Name NuGet `
                               -Source 'D:\PSRepo' `
                               -Force

       # Finally install Az from the local repo:
       Install-Module -Name Az `
                      -Repository LocalPSRepo `
                      -Force
       ```

    d. (Option B) If youâd rather skip registering a PSRepository, you can simply copy the module folders directly into one of your PSModulePath locations (e.g. C:\Program Files\WindowsPowerShell\Modules or %USERPROFILE%\Documents\WindowsPowerShell\Modules):

       ```powershell
       Copy-Item -Path 'D:\PSRepo\*' `
                 -Destination 'C:\Program Files\WindowsPowerShell\Modules' `
                 -Recurse -Force
       ```

       After that you can simply do:

       ```powershell
       Import-Module Az
       ```
